<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <DatadogArtifact>dd-sdk-android-trace-api</DatadogArtifact>
    <DatadogPackageName>TraceApi</DatadogPackageName>
    <Description>.NET for Android / .NET MAUI bindings for the native Datadog Android SDK's dd-sdk-android-trace-api module: the tracing contract - DatadogTracer, DatadogSpan, DatadogSpanContext and DatadogScope - that DatadogNet.Trace.Android implements. Built against dd-sdk-android $(DatadogNativeVersion). Arrives as a dependency of DatadogNet.Trace.Android; reference it directly only to code against the interfaces.</Description>
    <PackageTags>datadog-trace;apm;tracing</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="../Datadog.Binding.props" />

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../DatadogNet.Core.Android/DatadogNet.Core.Android.csproj"
                      JavaArtifact="com.datadoghq:dd-sdk-android-core:$(DatadogNativeVersion)" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../DatadogNet.Internal.Android/DatadogNet.Internal.Android.csproj"
                      JavaArtifact="com.datadoghq:dd-sdk-android-internal:$(DatadogNativeVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="GoogleGson" Version="$(GsonVersion)" />
    <PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Annotation" Version="$(AndroidXAnnotationVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    JCTools (lock-free queues) and RE2/J (linear-time regex) are the tracing engine's two
    third-party Java dependencies. Neither has a .NET binding on nuget.org, so both are embedded
    here as plain Java, exactly as they would arrive from Gradle. Bind="false": no C# consumer
    calls them, they only have to exist at runtime.

    They are embedded in THIS package and nowhere else, even though dd-sdk-android-trace-internal
    and dd-sdk-android-trace declare them too. The same classes contributed by three packages would
    be dexed three times and fail the consuming app's build with a duplicate class error, so those
    two projects take a dependency on this package and declare the pair satisfied instead. This is
    also why TraceInternal depends on TraceApi here when Maven says it does not: the edge exists to
    make one package the single owner of these jars.

    jctools-core is published with 'bundle' packaging - an OSGi bundle, which is a .jar with extra
    manifest entries - so it is consumed as a normal jar.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <DatadogMavenArtifact Include="org.jctools:jctools-core" Version="$(JctoolsVersion)" Bind="false" Packaging="jar" />
    <DatadogMavenArtifact Include="com.google.re2j:re2j" Version="$(Re2jVersion)" Bind="false" Packaging="jar" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
